<!--
  Detection-as-Code: native Wazuh twins for Linux detections.

  Contract: wazuh/rule_map.yml binds each rule here to its Sigma source by
  UUID + MITRE technique; tests/test_rule_map.py fails CI if they drift.

  Built-in chain these rules extend (confirmed against the live manager,
  0095-sshd_rules.xml):
    5700 -> sshd base
    5710 -> "Attempt to login using a non-existent user"  (level 5, T1110.001)
    5712 -> built-in brute force, frequency 8 / 120s      (level 10, T1110)
    5715 -> "authentication success"                      (level 3, benign)
    5716 -> "authentication failed" (EXISTING account)    (level 5, T1110)
    5720 -> built-in "multiple authentication failures",
            if_matched_sid 5716, frequency 8, same_source_ip (level 10, T1110)

  The 5710 and 5716 chains carry different evidence and must not be conflated:
  5710 fires when the account does NOT exist (enumeration); 5716 fires when the
  account EXISTS and the password was wrong (a real credential attack).

  Custom ID allocation: blocks of 10 per Sigma rule.
    10020x - ssh_brute_force   (sigma 0471bd9a-...)
    10021x - ssh_password_spray (sigma b7e4d1c8-...)
-->
<group name="dac,linux,syslog,">

  <!--
    Twin of rules/linux/credential_access/ssh_brute_force.yml
    (sigma id 0471bd9a-9b43-4f31-860f-4f8bf4950058).

    The Sigma rule expresses the atomic event (one invalid-user failure); the
    repetition threshold is applied here, where Wazuh's correlation engine
    lives. We alert at 6 failures from the same source in 120s - deliberately
    tighter than the built-in 5712 (8), so we catch a slow guesser earlier.

    ignore="60" suppresses re-alerting on the same source for 60s, matching
    the built-in's anti-spam behaviour.
  -->
  <rule id="100200" level="12" frequency="6" timeframe="120" ignore="60">
    <if_matched_sid>5710</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>DAC: SSH brute force - 6+ invalid-user failures from $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
      <id>T1110.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,credential_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!--
    Twin of rules/linux/credential_access/ssh_password_spray.yml
    (sigma id b7e4d1c8-3a92-4f6b-8d15-2c9e0a7f4b63).

    The Sigma rule expresses the atomic event (one failure against an EXISTING
    account); the breadth threshold is applied here, where Wazuh's correlation
    engine lives.

    Spray is breadth, not depth: <different_user /> is the load-bearing option.
    Without it this would be an ordinary volume rule and could not tell "one
    account hammered 5 times" from "5 accounts touched once each".

    Why this is additive rather than a duplicate of the built-in 5720:
      5720 = if_matched_sid 5716 + same_source_ip, frequency 8, NO user
      condition. A spray of 5 accounts x 1 attempt each is only 5 failures, so
      5720 never fires. This rule catches it at 5 distinct accounts.
    Chain is disjoint from 100200 by construction: 100200 hangs off 5710
    (non-existent accounts), this hangs off 5716 (real accounts).

    Level 12 matches 100200 and sits above the built-in 5720 (level 10) so a
    breadth finding is not buried under generic failure volume.
    ignore="60" suppresses re-alerting on the same source for 60s.
  -->
  <rule id="100210" level="12" frequency="5" timeframe="120" ignore="60">
    <if_matched_sid>5716</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <different_user />
    <description>DAC: SSH password spray - 5+ distinct accounts from $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
      <id>T1110.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,credential_access,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
